  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>ML = programming by example: data in, rules learned into parameters.</li>
      <li>Four parts: data (features + labels), model <Math tex="f_\theta" />, loss <Math tex="L" />, optimizer. Loop: predict → loss → update.</li>
      <li>House line: <Math tex="\hat{y} = wx + b" />, MSE loss; best fit for the three houses is <Math tex="w = 180,\ b \approx 23.3" /> (MSE 22.2).</li>
      <li>Goal is generalization. Train/validation/test. Test set is touched once. Training loss is not evidence.</li>
      <li>Overfit = memorized noise (low train, high test). Underfit = too simple (high both).</li>
      <li>Supervised (regression: number; classification: category), unsupervised (clusters, PCA), self-supervised (next token = LLM pretraining), reinforcement (rewards).</li>
      <li>Parameters learned; hyperparameters chosen and tuned on validation. Loss optimized; metric reported. Training sets θ; inference uses θ. Epoch = full pass; step = one update.</li>
      <li>Workflow: metric → split → baseline → train → evaluate held-out → iterate one change → deploy and monitor.</li>
      <li>AI ⊃ ML ⊃ DL ⊃ LLMs. Same four parts everywhere. Classical ML still wins on tables.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
